WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.472 --> 00:00:04.040
In a recent episode of
"Curb Your Enthusiasm,"

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.490
Larry David holds up a pack of cigarettes.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.890
Specifically, Morley cigarettes.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.800
Now, let's rewind back to 1960

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.557
and the Alfred Hitchcock classic "Psycho."

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.240
Take a look at the cigarettes

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.823
the psychiatrist is smoking

00:00:21.823 --> 00:00:23.810
in the final scene of the film.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:24.863
Look familiar?

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.580
Morleys have appeared in
hundreds of TV shows and movies

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.330
throughout the last century,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.230
in everything from "The
Twilight Zone" to "Seinfeld."

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.746
And, to be clear,

00:00:36.746 --> 00:00:39.921
Morleys are not a real cigarette brand.

00:00:39.921 --> 00:00:43.155
So, what exactly is going on here?

00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:46.890
Is this some deep, secret
connected cinematic universe

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.021
that ties Norman Bates to George Costanza?

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.680
If you pay really close
attention to shows and movies,

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.480
you'll start to notice
many of the same objects

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.846
over and over again,

00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:02.321
like this newspaper.

00:01:02.321 --> 00:01:04.160
There are several fake brands

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.650
that cross over into other works,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.255
including Acme, Oceanic
Air, and Heisler Beer.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.660
But one of the most
reused brands is Morley.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.726
Jonathan Kuntz: Morley
cigarettes is the most

00:01:18.726 --> 00:01:22.160
prominent brand of fictional cigarettes

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.590
that has been used for the
past 60 years in films,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.740
television shows, and video games.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.733
Narrator: Jonathan Kuntz
says they come from

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:32.500
a company in Sun Valley, California.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.640
Kuntz: The Earl Hays Press
is a 100-year-old prop house

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.210
that supplies fake-brand
props to Hollywood.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.050
And they are the main
suppliers of Morley cigarettes.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.780
Narrator: According to its website,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.650
Earl Hays Press pretty
much sells fake everything,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.463
from chips and beer to
magazines and license plates.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.610
Morley's design is based
on the most popular

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.320
real cigarette brand in the United States,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.870
Marlboro, with its signature
red and white packaging.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:08.030
Commercial: Come to where the flavor is.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.400
Come to Marlboro Country.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.340
Narrator: Marlboros are
often nicknamed Marleys,

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.560
hence the fake name Morley.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.900
Earl Hays Press sells Morleys
in a few different varieties,

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.735
including Morley Lights,
as you can see here.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.270
But to understand why Morley
even exists in the first place,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.983
we have to go back and take a look

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.700
at the love-hate relationship

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.380
between tobacco companies and Hollywood.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.213
Granny: What's that?

00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:37.810
Jed: Winston cigarettes.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.950
Try one, Granny. They is first rate.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.460
Narrator: In the 1950s,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.640
you mostly had sponsors for TV and radio,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.057
where the advertising agencies paid for

00:02:47.057 --> 00:02:48.973
and produced the show.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.260
The characters even
used the same products.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.876
And many of these early advertisers were,

00:02:55.876 --> 00:02:56.884
you guessed it,

00:02:56.884 --> 00:02:58.217
tobacco companies.

00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:02.350
"I Love Lucy" was famously
sponsored by Philip Morris,

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.330
so you would see brand
integrations like this.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.150
Lucy: Have you tried a
Philip Morris lately?

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.040
Desi: You'll love 'em.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.210
They're so smooth and mild,
and they taste so good.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.800
Narrator: Movies were also
typically cross-promoted

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.360
with tobacco companies.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.690
Here's Joan Crawford, for example,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.880
smoking Chesterfield
cigarettes in a poster

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.590
promoting her film "Flamingo Road."

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.850
If a show wasn't sponsored,

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.360
it would use a fake brand instead,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.600
since it wasn't getting
paid to show the real brand.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.800
Enter Morley. This fake
brand can be traced

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.200
all the way back to
episodes of "Perry Mason"

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.397
and one of the most memorable
horror movies of all time,

00:03:41.397 --> 00:03:42.950
"Psycho,"

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.050
although the box shows
an earlier white design

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.600
as opposed to the newer
red and white version.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.160
Kuntz: And I think this
is what really started

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.920
the Morley cigarettes craze.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.917
Narrator: Morleys popped up in TV shows

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:59.830
like "Naked City" and "The Twilight Zone,"

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.100
most famously "Nightmare at 20,000 Feet,"

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.120
with William Shatner lighting
up a Morley on a plane.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.986
But Morleys weren't used as much

00:04:08.986 --> 00:04:11.710
during the 1970s and 1980s.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.543
Why?

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:16.760
For one, the 1970 Public
Health Cigarette Smoking Act

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.800
banned cigarette ads from
airing on television and radio.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.130
This was part of a big push
to stop smoking in TV shows.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.513
And the appearance of cigarettes on TV

00:04:27.513 --> 00:04:29.854
decreased in general around this time

00:04:29.854 --> 00:04:32.260
by about 70%.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.790
Tobacco companies turned
to movies instead,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.340
paying big bucks to have
their cigarettes appear

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.980
in popular films throughout the '80s.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.646
So there wasn't much of a need

00:04:42.646 --> 00:04:44.600
for fake brands like Morley

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.250
in movies either.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.520
But this all changed again in 1998

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.980
with what's known as the
Master Settlement Agreement,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.130
which banned tobacco advertising
in both films and TV,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.597
paving the way for the fake
brand's triumphant return.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.390
Kuntz: The real explosion
of Morley cigarettes

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.339
in popularity in pop culture

00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:16.960
comes at the end of the
1990s in "The X-Files."

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.510
Scully: Well, you'll be interested to know

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.110
that Morley Tobacco has subpoenaed

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.230
all of our files of the case.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.205
Narrator: In the show,
The Cigarette Smoking Man

00:05:25.205 --> 00:05:28.253
is a villainous character
played by William Davis.

00:05:28.253 --> 00:05:30.700
He famously smoked Morleys.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.940
And the fake tobacco company
was actually incorporated

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.910
into the plot in the
episode called "Brand X,"

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.110
something you couldn't do
very easily with a real brand.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.130
But more on that in a bit.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:44.796
Following "The X-Files,"

00:05:44.796 --> 00:05:47.662
Morley started showing up everywhere,

00:05:47.662 --> 00:05:51.260
even in video games and music videos.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.970
Kuntz: It has literally
appeared in hundreds

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.870
of movies and television shows.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.550
Narrator: Here's George
smoking a pack in "Seinfeld."

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.820
And Chandler holding a pack
in an episode of "Friends."

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.760
They're also found in "Justified"

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.267
and the 2019 "Breaking
Bad" movie "El Camino."

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.030
Shows technically have the freedom

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:14.590
to show real brands these
days for historical realism,

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.760
as long as it's not a paid ad.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.780
Don Draper: Lucky Strikes is toasted.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.270
Narrator: So, why would they opt

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:24.010
for a made-up brand from the '60s?

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.380
Well, real brands are a big risk.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.420
Kuntz: There are several reasons
why film and TV producers

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:35.330
would avoid using real brand
names without permission.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.760
There is a little bit of legal jeopardy

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.580
if your product is used to kill somebody

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.570
or is defamed in any way.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.990
You might be subject to lawsuits.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.220
Narrator: A study found
that villains are the ones

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.750
who are typically depicted
smoking in films and TV.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.060
And brands likely do not want their image

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.070
associated with criminals.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.845
Apple, for instance, reportedly

00:06:56.845 --> 00:07:00.070
doesn't let villains use
its products in movies.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:03.060
Real products are typically
covered up or disguised

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.390
when used in shows or movies.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:08.412
In the 1930s and '40s, the brand letters

00:07:08.412 --> 00:07:10.930
on cars in films were carefully removed.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.300
Kuntz: Hollywood was quite careful

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.390
to keep brand names out of their films.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:16.780
They didn't want to be associated

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.360
with any particular product line,

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.970
and they didn't want to offend the people

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.780
that were using a rival product.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.960
Narrator: So these days,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.670
Morleys are mainly used
as the replacement brand,

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.130
an inside joke,

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.760
similar to those 555 phone numbers

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.496
and the Wilhelm scream.

00:07:32.496 --> 00:07:34.210
[screams]

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:35.980
Kuntz: Morley cigarettes have become

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.430
almost like a meme on the web,

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.880
something where you can
kind of wink your eye

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.327
at the audience and say,

00:07:42.327 --> 00:07:44.039
you know, "We know that you know

00:07:44.039 --> 00:07:46.347
that this really a movie
or a television show,

00:07:46.347 --> 00:07:48.660
and we're just kind of
playing with things."

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:49.960
Narrator: And just as there's a risk

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.430
associated with smoking real cigarettes,

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.940
there's a risk with Morleys as well.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:59.240
Kuntz: The problem with the
popularity of Morley cigarettes

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.520
is that when you see it now

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.340
in a movie or in a television show,

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:05.573
it kind of pulls you out of the fiction.

